{"causes": ["The exponential growth of written materials, especially after the invention of the printing press, necessitated structured methods for managing and retrieving information.", "The establishment of large institutional libraries, such as the Library of Alexandria, created an urgent need for comprehensive catalogs to organize their vast collections.", "The scholarly pursuit of knowledge required reliable methods for identifying, authenticating, and citing sources, leading to the development of critical and analytical bibliography."], "effects": ["Bibliography enabled the systematic organization and retrieval of information, forming the basis for modern library science and information architecture.", "It provided scholars with essential tools for verifying the authenticity, provenance, and textual integrity of historical documents and literary works.", "The principles of bibliographic control laid the groundwork for universal classification systems and digital metadata standards, profoundly impacting the digital age of information."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "paul-otlet", "sourceName": "Paul Otlet", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "bibliography", "targetName": "bibliography", "context": "Paul Otlet, alongside Henri La Fontaine, was instrumental in creating the modern discipline of documentation, a direct evolution of systematic bibliography, in the late 19th and early 20th centuries."}, {"sourceSlug": "printing-press", "sourceName": "Printing Press", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "bibliography", "targetName": "bibliography", "context": "Gutenberg's invention of the printing press in the 1440s dramatically increased book production, thereby causing an urgent need for more sophisticated bibliographic methods to manage the burgeoning volume of texts."}, {"sourceSlug": "library-of-alexandria", "sourceName": "Library of Alexandria", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "alexandria-egypt", "targetName": "Alexandria, Egypt", "context": "The early, monumental bibliographic work of Callimachus's *Pinakes* occurred in the Library of Alexandria, Egypt, around the 3rd century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "callimachus", "sourceName": "Callimachus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "bibliography", "targetName": "bibliography", "context": "Callimachus participated in the foundational development of systematic bibliography by compiling the *Pinakes*, a 120-volume catalog of Greek literature at the Library of Alexandria in the 3rd century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "universal-decimal-classification", "sourceName": "Universal Decimal Classification", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "bibliography", "targetName": "bibliography", "context": "The Universal Decimal Classification, developed by Otlet and La Fontaine, transmits the core principles of systematic organization and indexing central to modern bibliography."}], "places": [{"name": "Alexandria, Egypt", "role": "Early Development"}, {"name": "Brussels, Belgium", "role": "Modern Foundation"}, {"name": "Mainz, Germany", "role": "Catalyst for Growth"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: bibliography", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "bibliography"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q134995", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bibliography"], "tags": ["academic discipline", "Technological Innovations", "scientific-paradigm"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "bibliography left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 84 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Influenced intellectual traditions and cultural development.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Bibliography has fundamentally shaped human civilization by providing the essential methods for organizing and accessing knowledge across millennia, directly enabling the growth of scholarship and the modern information age. Its evolution from ancient catalogs to digital databases has consistently facilitated intellectual progress and cultural transmission.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T21:33:59Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30400763721", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Academic discipline that studies books Founded by Paul Otlet.", "newValue": "Bibliography, the systematic study and cataloging of books and other textual materials, originated in antiquity as a practical necessity for managing vast collections of written knowledge, evolving into a distinct academic discipline foundational to scholarship. Its earliest manifestations appear in the 3rd century BCE with Callimachus's *Pinakes* at the Library of Alexandria, an ambitious 120-volume catalog of Greek literature, demonstrating an early awareness of the need to organize and describe textual output. This foundational practice of listing texts has since underpinned the preservation, dissemination, and critical analysis of human intellectual heritage across diverse civilizations.

The development of bibliography intensified with the advent of printing in the 15th century, which dramatically increased the volume of available texts and, consequently, the complexity of their management. Figures like Konrad Gesner, with his 1545 *Bibliotheca universalis*, created comprehensive enumerative bibliographies listing all known Latin, Greek, and Hebrew books. The discipline further formalized in the late 19th and early 20th centuries through the work of pioneers like Paul Otlet and Henri La Fontaine, who established the International Institute of Bibliography (IIB) in Brussels in 1895, aiming for a universal bibliographic control system based on the Universal Decimal Classification (UDC).

The enduring legacy of bibliography is evident in every library catalog, database, and academic citation system, providing the essential infrastructure for accessing and evaluating information. It underpins all scholarly research by establishing the authenticity, provenance, and relationships between texts, thus ensuring the integrity of historical and scientific inquiry. From the handwritten scrolls of ancient Egypt to the digital metadata of the modern internet, the principles of bibliography remain indispensable for navigating the ever-expanding universe of human knowledge."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-28T21:33:59Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30400763721", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Intellectual inquiry and cultural development"], "newValue": ["The exponential growth of written materials, especially after the invention of the printing press, necessitated structured methods for managing and retrieving information.", "The establishment of large institutional libraries, such as the Library of Alexandria, created an urgent need for comprehensive catalogs to organize their vast collections.", "The scholarly pursuit of knowledge required reliable methods for identifying, authenticating, and citing sources, leading to the development of critical and analytical bibliography."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-28T21:33:59Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30400763721", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Influenced intellectual traditions and cultural development"], "newValue": ["Bibliography enabled the systematic organization and retrieval of information, forming the basis for modern library science and information architecture.", "It provided scholars with essential tools for verifying the authenticity, provenance, and textual integrity of historical documents and literary works.", "The principles of bibliographic control laid the groundwork for universal classification systems and digital metadata standards, profoundly impacting t